$DIR/main.p:19:8: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with go: wg lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the puroutine runs (spec section 12.12); wg has type stdsync.WaitGroup, which must not be copied, so start the literal with a stdsync.Scope, which waits for it and lets it use wg: var s stdsync.Scope; s.Task(func() {...})
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A go statement may start it after that
    function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for
    it before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or
    a WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments:
    they are copied when the go statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a WaitGroup
    or another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.
    wg holds a value of type stdsync.WaitGroup. Passing it as an argument would
    copy it, and a copy is a separate value: calling Done or Unlock on the copy
    never reaches the original, so a Wait or Lock on the original may never
    return. A sync.Scope declared in this function waits for the tasks it starts
    before its block ends, so they may use wg where it is.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    11.5).
    Start the literal with a stdsync.Scope:
        var s stdsync.Scope
        s.Task(func() {
            ...
        })
    Or declare it at package level and pass a pointer:
        var wg stdsync.WaitGroup // at package level
        task func(wg *stdsync.WaitGroup, i int) {
            ...
        }(&wg, i)
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2590" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:7: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with go: s lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the puroutine runs (spec section 12.12); s has type stats, which holds a value of type stdsync.Mutex and must not be copied, so start the literal with a stdsync.Scope, which waits for it and lets it use s: var sc stdsync.Scope; sc.Task(func() {...})
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A go statement may start it after that
    function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for
    it before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or
    a WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments:
    they are copied when the go statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a WaitGroup
    or another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.
    s holds a value of type stdsync.Mutex. Passing it as an argument would copy
    it, and a copy is a separate value: calling Done or Unlock on the copy never
    reaches the original, so a Wait or Lock on the original may never return. A
